] [17592186115517 #edb/ref 1074 "To graph the function $y=f(\\frac{1}{2}x),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and stretch it by factor of $2$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $2.$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/15/15b9b0530dbd4a193efb2ffd6d7719472c6aa641714298c4644ae47d167cf175.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116156 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116156 #edb/ref 1061 18031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116156 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c0285e1d-13e9-58ab-bba4-cfc15f23ca69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116156 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116157 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116157 #edb/ref 1061 18032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116157 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f833b0bd-73c5-5268-9213-0c6017d44609" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116157 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116158 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116158 #edb/ref 1061 18034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116158 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e73f013f-5338-5d6d-9d71-6eed3918d6ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116158 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116159 #edb/ref 1061 18035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116159 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "708280f8-6071-589c-9998-87930ecb29a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116159 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116506 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the function ![](images/44/44c838deb3fa8e3182fbd9d4a03f964dc2731730559d00328190e9259bf01762.svg) whose graph is shown below. How can we plot the graph of ![](images/12/120fd26f32d53872d244163d5a4d63931d0f881b62f3f5f7a924a9c06255ba1d.svg)\n\n![](images/1a/1a11bef66d0529418dde00e5713ed5e98f53a948c8a60f83fd056fd9e2c18698.png)\n\nWe can use some points on the graph of ![](images/44/44c838deb3fa8e3182fbd9d4a03f964dc2731730559d00328190e9259bf01762.svg) to find the corresponding points on the graphs of ![](images/a1/a142ed820c2bdafb8c1b7969028c7f4533c66fa772a73954c46f6b5eb91a050f.svg)\n\nFor example, let's take the point ![](images/8f/8f231df171077c673ad3f7611cfd99a5fb2753448232eb64a5a160a42a21a122.svg) The fact that it lies on the graph of ![](images/44/44c838deb3fa8e3182fbd9d4a03f964dc2731730559d00328190e9259bf01762.svg) means ![](images/53/5327a1cd77a2b8abb05cd13fa3d2c65cdd0a7ff447afd71e682a0282118e886c.svg) Then, if ![](images/06/068f771676e51ca5f58fb024dd4635c8da494dc49bd43398585945ce0920a425.svg) we have\n![](images/03/033afdad9ff42ad24f2d0f4388e6065adea3608bf49116364b5c13833ded06cf.svg) \n\nThis means the point ![](images/b2/b2f8b859ae7e2b4fe083670dc262b473bd79b7b1ee50a60712ae5bc13bffb954.svg) that lies on ![](images/44/44c838deb3fa8e3182fbd9d4a03f964dc2731730559d00328190e9259bf01762.svg) has a corresponding point ![](images/e2/e2e718c0eb1b8cf9581404873ddc1cc4512141d32e2fb42b70f516257ec31365.svg) that lies on ![](images/a1/a142ed820c2bdafb8c1b7969028c7f4533c66fa772a73954c46f6b5eb91a050f.svg)\n\nSo, for each point on the graph of ![](images/c9/c956e6cbfb2d94fa56a68a3173ec78f60fee33a9066c9c44ca4096d67d7b1cba.svg) there will be a point on the graph of ![](images/b9/b9b8d001be0277e544ff2d040e5b537ad8d7df49d33027a56021f4ba62497de3.svg) with the *same* ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-value and *one half* the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-value:\n\n![](images/66/66e2a4dfc65fc35cd4b812ea418df3976c6e23cc002babaadd1921745353de6b.svg)\n\nFinally, we can plot the points and draw the graph of ![](images/29/2994084d895116512b0d26509c9108d059fa2e73bbc4ea785e8535fce4a7f75b.svg)\n\n![](images/8c/8ce6a8fd398025bfb9cd535fbc16db06a2a219e0d2451cf6278d81d4ab5c264a.png)\n\nThe graph of ![](images/b9/b9b8d001be0277e544ff2d040e5b537ad8d7df49d33027a56021f4ba62497de3.svg) is the graph of ![](images/44/44c838deb3fa8e3182fbd9d4a03f964dc2731730559d00328190e9259bf01762.svg) **horizontally scaled** by a factor of ![](images/d7/d7cae033e26adc59eeceb95b5807505dd0242e42982f350c36209152864cf261.svg)\n\nEquivalently, we can say that the original graph is **compressed** by a scale factor of ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg)\n\nIn general, the transformation ![](images/45/45950fa47c3a14fa07312805dd06e2638388335b9781c6ba1c07c76d7a5b108c.svg) scales the graph of ![](images/44/44c838deb3fa8e3182fbd9d4a03f964dc2731730559d00328190e9259bf01762.svg) horizontally by a factor of ![](images/aa/aad37c78cd66cde5ccbba8aa66a6d18afcb0fd5b7de52471203a690e0645e4c5.svg)\n\n- If ![](images/8d/8dc6f7e80ecaac9fa07890ce1881dd51de319597ae8b1dce821aa03c1f3eff06.svg) is greater than ![](images/76/76543f4805f22715457d55feb2a3c87d0d873bdcb9c1eed7a4f68539269dcf59.svg) the graph is compressed (or squished) towards the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis by a scale factor of ![](images/c7/c7432bf7a520e569e53fdd1a7303ce6b9f940d99e68b5e7ea3196c51acfe2b69.svg)\n\n- If ![](images/8d/8dc6f7e80ecaac9fa07890ce1881dd51de319597ae8b1dce821aa03c1f3eff06.svg) is between ![](images/15/156b31ede73de5301befe67f02745a0202f1806a62f9fdecc6fc9ee3f21117ba.svg) and ![](images/76/76543f4805f22715457d55feb2a3c87d0d873bdcb9c1eed7a4f68539269dcf59.svg) the graph is stretched away from the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis by a scale factor of ![](images/c7/c7432bf7a520e569e53fdd1a7303ce6b9f940d99e68b5e7ea3196c51acfe2b69.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116506 #edb/ref 1065 3285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116506 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0f9522aa-685f-5492-a439-69c534d5bbef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116506 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529630 #edb/ref 1080 "imag