$ that must lie on $y=f(x).$\n\nThe coordinates of $Q$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530449 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4862c2b5-a520-5c18-b89e-ce5de629850e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530449 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x)=f(3\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}x)),$ the function $y=f(x)$ represents a compression of the function $y=f(\\frac{1}{3}x)$ by a factor of $3$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of dividing the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(\\frac{1}{3}x)$ by $3.$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-15,6)$ on $f(\\frac{1}{3}x)$ is mapped to the point $(\\frac{-15}{3},6)=(-5,6),$ and this point must lie on the graph of $y=f(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530451 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530451 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112072" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530451 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530451 #edb/ref 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(18,-12)$ lies on the curve $y=f(\\frac{1}{6}x).$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=f(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530451 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "49ad3904-35d2-53e9-8532-90b9810959eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530451 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x)=f(6\\cdot (\\frac{1}{6}x)),$ the function $y=f(x)$ represents a compression of the function $y=f(\\frac{1}{6}x)$ by a factor of $6$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of dividing the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(\\frac{1}{6}x)$ by $6.$\n\nTherefore, the point $(18,-12)$ on $f(\\frac{1}{6}x)$ is mapped to the point $(\\frac{18}{6},-12)=(3,-12),$ and this point must lie on the graph of $y=f(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530471 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530471 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530471 #edb/ref 1069 "q-249484" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530471 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530471 #edb/ref 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(2,-5)$ lies on the curve $y=f(7x).$ Find the point $Q$ that must lie on $y=f(x).$\n\nThe coordinates of $Q$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530471 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6bebb2ef-00e1-59ff-ace6-82ab8feb5573" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530471 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x)=f(\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot (7x)),$ the function $y=f(x)$ represents a stretch of the function $y=f(7x)$ by a factor of $7$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(7x)$ by $7.$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,-5)$ on $f(7x)$ is mapped to the point $(7\\cdot 2,-5)=(14,-5),$ and this point must lie on the graph of $y=f(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530501 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530501 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111623" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530501 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530501 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/86/869e65cb2894403a5fe9f5e5f68e1c7882f8c86aff7596e557958e1f6cbbe348.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(3x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530501 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a52aedb3-cb6c-5591-aec7-501eba1e68dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530501 #edb/ref 1074 "To graph the function $y=f(3x),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and compress it by factor of $3$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of dividing the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $3.$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/b8/b84a3ca0b17949c07748e73235d026d696c199136e448b32639959036c5389a8.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530509 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530509 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112065" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530509 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530509 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/75/750ef4e4dc4c70ccbd9f7b30ca7c15e996535d55daf95e6eeeaaf3f096b3cf15.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{5}x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530509 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ac83ae74-1a8c-543b-99e8-3815a1447e2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530509 #edb/ref 1074 "To graph the function $y=f(\\frac{1}{5}x),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and stretch it by factor of $5$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $5.$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/8f/8fb2bcfc545d9a6b9cf316b758d82a96c8807169ab345cb3cf8cce03d456c50e.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530516 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530516 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79225" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530516 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530516 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c9/c91b2bd90465b05e7fe1fe605f1ee37d4ac81200f3bb4d51922eedd188316eba.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(5x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530516 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c23af42c-b294-5f6c-af96-07b0ad73f00b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530516 #edb/ref 1074 "To graph the function $y=f(5x),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and compress it by factor of $5$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of dividing the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $5.$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f8bd94b2652d96417690f4ddb67a3d6c3235dd828c98b9f0ddebf0cf2fb13c51.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530523 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530523 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530523 #edb/ref 1069 "q-249480" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530523 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530523 #edb/ref 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(5,3)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Find the point $Q$ that must lie on $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}x).$\n\nThe coordinates of $Q$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530523 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c6ba2a58-fad3-5500-859d-8acd4ee3f5d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530523 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}x)$ represents a stretch of the function $y=f(x)$ by a factor of $4$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $4.$\n\nTherefore, the point $(5,3)$ on $f(x)$ is mapped to the point $(4\\cdot 5,3)=(20,3),$ and this point must lie on the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}x)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
